WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:09.320
English, I'm Alex Villareal.

00:09.320 --> 00:14.080
This is the VOA Special English Technology Report.

00:14.080 --> 00:20.000
Sony officials held a news conference in early May in Tokyo.

00:20.000 --> 00:27.000
They apologized for the theft of personal information from millions of users of the

00:27.000 --> 00:34.360
company's online services. Hackers targeted Sony's PlayStation Network, Sony

00:34.360 --> 00:43.520
Online Entertainment, and Curiosity Music Systems. In all, Sony says information may

00:43.520 --> 00:51.920
have been stolen from more than 100 million accounts. The data includes

00:51.920 --> 01:01.000
information like names, addresses, phone numbers, and birth dates. In some cases, even

01:01.000 --> 01:08.160
credit and debit card numbers may have been stolen. The United States Federal

01:08.160 --> 01:16.840
Bureau of Investigation is looking into the cyber attacks. Sony shut down its

01:16.840 --> 01:26.000
PlayStation Network on April 20th. The company is being criticized for not telling

01:26.000 --> 01:35.440
its users about the stolen information until a week later. Sony has also been

01:35.440 --> 01:43.000
criticized for not making its accounts more secure by encrypting the data.

01:43.760 --> 01:52.440
Sony officials say they have now taken steps to improve security. Peter Warren is

01:52.440 --> 01:59.880
chairman of the Cyber Security Research Institute in London. His group studies

01:59.880 --> 02:07.560
technology crimes and their effects on our computer-dependent world. Mr.

02:07.600 --> 02:15.840
Warren points out that what happened at Sony is not rare. We've seen a number of

02:15.840 --> 02:22.880
very large companies that have suffered some tremendously embarrassing losses of

02:22.880 --> 02:30.560
data. The problem, he says, is that there is far more interest in developing

02:30.560 --> 02:39.240
technology than in keeping it safe. As he puts it, lots and lots of people are

02:39.240 --> 02:46.160
being allowed to connect a lot of technology up before it's really been

02:46.160 --> 02:55.240
particularly well tested. Peter Warren also points out that many people have

02:55.360 --> 03:03.360
a false sense of security online. On the internet, he says, they seem to be so

03:03.360 --> 03:11.520
blindly trusting of anybody and of any website, no matter how suspicious it may

03:11.520 --> 03:21.520
look. His message to users is simple. Try to be more careful on websites. He

03:21.520 --> 03:28.720
says, the internet is just like any other street and there are some twists and

03:28.720 --> 03:38.080
turns that can take you into some very, very unsavory places. For VOA Special

03:38.080 --> 03:45.320
English, I'm Alex Villareal. To read and listen to more technology news and

03:46.040 --> 03:54.680
for English teaching activities, go to voaspecialenglish.com. You can also follow

03:54.680 --> 04:00.840
us on Twitter and like us on Facebook at VOA Learning English.

